---
/* THE NICOTRA — the maternal Sicilian line, as arena.astro is for the Arena.
 *
 * Three days ago this line was two names on a Queensland death registration
 * and nothing else: «John Nicotra» and «Venera Cassaneti», parents of Maria,
 * with no dates, no places and no act. It now runs to generation six on both
 * sides and crosses four comuni — and the man it is named for is still lost.
 *
 * The page is built to hold BOTH halves of that: what four acts established,
 * and what twenty-seven read years have failed to.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import additions from "../data/additions.json";
import nic from "../data/nicotra.json";

const adds = additions.filter((a) => /Nicotra|Cassaniti|Murabito|Currenti/.test(a.name));


const LINE = nic.line;
const total = nic.read;
---
<Base title="The Nicotra" eyebrow="Mascali · Giarre · Riposto · Piedimonte Etneo"
      dek="Three days ago this line was two names on a Queensland death certificate. It now reaches generation six on both sides, crosses four comuni — and the man it is named for is still missing."

>
  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Where it started</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Everything this archive knew about the Nicotra came from one Australian document: the
      <strong>1963 Queensland death registration</strong> of <P n="Maria Nicotra" />, which names her
      parents as <strong>«John Nicotra»</strong> and <strong>«Venera Cassaneti»</strong>. No dates, no
      places, no act — and one of those surnames <strong>does not exist in Italy</strong>. «Cassaneti»
      appears exactly once in the whole Queensland index, on that certificate, taken down from an
      informant at a Brisbane registry counter. The Sicilian form is
      <strong>CASSANITI</strong>, and a register searched for the other spelling returns nothing
      forever.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The line, as four acts give it</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">Read in the original, September 2026. Not one of these
    people is in the family's export with a date.</p>
    <div class="prose">
      {LINE.map((r) => (
        <div style="margin-top:1.3rem;padding-left:1rem;border-left:3px solid var(--rule)">
          <div class="lab">generation {r.gen}</div>
          <h3 style="margin:.3rem 0 .4rem">{r.who}</h3>
          <p style="margin:0">{r.what}</p>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:.4rem;color:var(--ink-3)">{r.src}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>The join is not circular, which matters because
    «Giovanni Nicotra» is a common name.</strong> The family's own export — parsed long before any of
    this — gives Giovanni and Venera <strong>seven children, and one of them is a ROSARIO</strong>. A
    first son takes the paternal grandfather's name, and the paternal grandfather in the 1872 act is
    Rosario. This archive held the grandson's name for months and could not have derived the
    grandfather's from it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What {total} read years have not found</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">Rosario Nicotra and Venera Murabito are named in one
    act and appear in no register.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Comune</th><th>Series</th><th class="m">Years read</th><th>What it said</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {Object.entries(nic.byComuneSeries).map(([c, ser]) =>
            Object.entries(ser).map(([sname, rows]) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{c}</strong></td>
              <td class="small">{sname} {rows[0].year}–{rows[rows.length - 1].year}</td>
              <td class="m">{rows.filter((r) => r.entries !== "\u2014").length}</td>
              <td class="small">{rows.filter((r) => !["\u2014", "none", "empty"].includes(r.entries)).length
                ? `${rows.filter((r) => !["\u2014", "none", "empty"].includes(r.entries)).length} year(s) hold an N entry; none of them is Rosario`
                : "not one N entry in any year read"}</td>
            </tr>
          )))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>The instrument is not the problem.</strong> Every
    one of these Tavole is alphabetical and names both parents, so the household would announce itself.
    What is being tested is <strong>whether the man's own statements about himself are true</strong> —
    he is <em>trentuno</em> in 1872 and <em>ventotto</em> in 1875, six years apart in three — and
    whether «nato a Giarre» means the comune or the district.</p>
  </section>

  {adds.length ? (
  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The people these acts added</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th class="m">Gen</th><th class="m">Place</th><th>Trade</th><th class="m">In the tree</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {adds.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{a.name}</strong></td>
              <td class="m">{a.gen}</td>
              <td class="m small">{a.place}</td>
              <td class="small">{a.trade}</td>
              <td class="m small">{a.inTree && a.inTree !== "—" ? "yes" : <em>no</em>}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>) : null}

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">22 September 2026 · Riposto, Matrimoni 1844, atto 39</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The wrong Nicotra, and the right shape</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Twelve years of Riposto's marriage <em>Tavole</em> were read for exactly the row that would
      end this: Rosario Nicotra and Venera Murabito, who would hand over generation seven in a single
      line. <strong>That row is not there.</strong> What is there is one N entry across the twelve
      years, and it is a different man.</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:12px">
        <div class="k">Groom</div><div class="v"><strong>Nicotra Salvadore</strong></div><div class="w"><em>patria</em> <strong>GIARRE</strong></div>
        <div class="k">His parents</div><div class="v">Alfio Nicotra · Rosa Pappalardo</div><div class="w">no <em>Don</em>, no <em>Donna</em></div>
        <div class="k">Bride</div><div class="v">Barbagallo Maria</div><div class="w"><em>patria</em> Riposto</div>
        <div class="k">Her parents</div><div class="v">Rosario Barbagallo · Anna Campagna</div><div class="w">—</div>
        <div class="k">Married</div><div class="v">July 1844</div><div class="w">the day is not legible in the index</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:16px">He is the <strong>Salvadore Nicotra</strong> whose children the
      Riposto birth sweep had already turned up — Alfio in May 1845, Rosa in November 1847 — without
      knowing where he came from. Now it is written: <strong>born at Giarre, married at Riposto,
      children registered at Riposto.</strong></p>
      <p><strong>This is not Giovanni's father and this archive does not pretend it is.</strong> What
      it settles is the question behind the search. «Nato a Giarre» and «absent from Giarre's
      registers» stopped being a contradiction the moment a second man did both. Whether Rosario is
      Salvadore's brother is the obvious next thought and has <strong>no evidence at all</strong>
      behind it — two men of a surname in one district are not kin until an act says so, and this
      archive has been wrong about exactly that before.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Eight Nicotra households, and not one of them his</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">Forty-five read index years turned these up. They are
    kept because a surname that is <em>present</em> and never <em>right</em> is a different fact from
    a surname that is absent — and because the next reader will otherwise find them again.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Comune</th><th>Father</th><th>Mother</th><th class="m">Seen</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {nic.households.map((h) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="m small">{h.comune}</td>
              <td><strong>{h.father}</strong></td>
              <td class="small">{h.mother}</td>
              <td class="m small">{h.seen}</td>
              <td class="small">{h.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>None of these is a join and none is offered as
    one.</strong> Two of the fathers are called Alfio and a third is a Rosario, which is precisely the
    trap this archive has fallen into before — four undated Rosario Mazza were once fused on the name
    alone, and the rule since is that <strong>two people of a surname in one district are not kin
    until an act says so</strong>. <a href={u("/method/")}>The instruments →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>One search that cannot work, written down so nobody plans it twice</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The obvious move, when a man's birthplace is in doubt and his marriage bundle survives, is to
      open the bundle: a <em>processetto</em> carries <strong>both spouses' birth extracts</strong> and
      their dead parents' death acts, and would answer Giovanni Nicotra's birthplace in one image.</p>
      <p><strong>It cannot work here, and the reason is a change of régime.</strong> The Bourbon-era
      <em>Matrimoni, processetti</em> do carry those extracts, and this archive has mined them at
      Piedimonte and at Scilla. The Italian-era <em>Matrimoni, allegati</em> — which is what 1872 is —
      carry <strong>the publication certificate and little else: no birth extract, no death act</strong>.
      What they do carry, and the processetti do not, is the printed <em>vivente / fu</em> field that
      established Rosario Nicotra and Venera Murabito were both alive.</p>
      <p><strong>Fascicolo 20 of 1872 has been read, and it is already giving everything it has.</strong>
      A researcher reasoning from the processetti to the allegati will plan a search the volume cannot
      answer.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What would carry it further</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><strong>Salvadore Nicotra's own birth act at Giarre.</strong> He married in 1844, so he was
      born about 1815–1824 — a decade earlier than anything read here. That act names Alfio Nicotra's
      age and trade, and would say whether this Giarre household is a labouring one or the
      <em>Dottor Don</em> line under another spelling.</p>
      <p><strong>And the rest of Giarre's marriage Tavole.</strong> Mascali's are now read whole for
      1835–1841 and 1847, and Riposto's for 1835–1846 — twenty years of the one index that names both
      spouses, all four parents and the comune each came from, and Rosario is in none of them. Of
      Giarre's, only <strong>1840 and 1844</strong> have been read. The other nine years have their
      containers banked and are held up by nothing but a rate limiter.</p>
      <p><a href={u("/riposto/")}>Riposto, where the search stands →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/giarre/")}>Giarre, and its negative →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/mascali/")}>Mascali, and her birth act →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
